## 4. What good evidence looks like

The example discussions we've reviewed (the QA mock EPA video and post-EPA write-ups from completed apprentices https://shkspr.mobi/blog/2023/03/questions-asked-at-an-end-point-assessment-professional-discussion/) point to the same handful of habits:

**Workplace first, first person always.** "I designed", "I tested", "I raised it with the team". You can mention collaboration and validation with others, but always loop back to what you did individually. An assignment written in academic third person needs rewriting before it will work in a discussion.

**Anchor with a consistent label, state your role.** Internal security policy means you can't name projects specifically, and that's fine: the assessor needs to follow your story, not identify your systems. Describe each project by what it does, using the same label every time: "a customer-facing payments service", "an internal case-management system I'll call the casework project". Agree with your line manager what level of description is allowed, decide your labels in advance, use the same ones your submitted portfolio used where it has them, and practise them until they come out naturally. A consistent label lets the assessor connect your answers across the discussion just as well as a real name would; what breaks an answer is fumbling mid-sentence over what you're allowed to say.

**Use the KSB vocabulary.** If the KSB says "security and resilience techniques", say those words and then name your specific techniques (threat modelling, dependency scanning, graceful degradation, backups and restore testing). This makes it unmistakable which KSB you're evidencing.

**In the discussion, evidence is spoken.** Screenshots, emails, and artefacts did their job in the submitted portfolio; you won't be producing anything physical during the discussion. Your evidence on the day is specific, first-hand description: what you did, with what, in what order, and what changed as a result. "I introduced retry logic on the integration layer and the overnight failure rate dropped noticeably" is spoken evidence; "it went well" is not.

**Short beats long.** Aim for roughly 40 words per section, around 200 words per answer. The portfolio helps you talk; the talking is what's assessed.

**Know the difference between K, S, and B.** A useful framing: *Knowledge* is "I can prove that I know...", *Skill* is "I can prove that I'm able to...", and *Behaviour* is "I can prove that I consistently...". Behaviours in particular need workplace evidence, since you can't demonstrate consistency in a one-off assignment.

