## 8. The reusable prompt

Give this prompt to each apprentice to run with an AI assistant (Claude or similar). They should attach or paste in: (1) their portfolio of evidence, (2) the workplace evidence supplied to them, and (3) they can leave the KSB table below in place, since it's already included. The prompt does the audit, pair, and rewrite steps from Section 6 and produces a navigable evidence bank plus a gap report.

```text
You are helping me prepare for my Level 6 Digital and Technology Solutions
Professional (Software Engineering pathway) EPA Professional Discussion.

I will provide two sources of evidence:

SOURCE 1 - MY PORTFOLIO: mostly university assignment-based evidence.
SOURCE 2 - WORKPLACE EVIDENCE: my workplace promotion evidence, written
against my organisation's internal competency framework. It is organised
by competency indicators, not KSBs, so part of your job is translating
it: map each competency indicator's evidence to the KSBs it supports,
and preserve the original indicator reference for traceability.

THE ASSESSMENT
The professional discussion is a structured two-way conversation with an
independent assessor. I do not know the exact questions in advance, only
that they explore four themes built from the KSB wording below. I must
answer in the first person about real workplace activity, and I may refer
to my portfolio during the discussion.

MY PORTFOLIO IS ALREADY SUBMITTED and cannot be changed. The independent
assessor prepares their questions from that submitted portfolio, which
is mostly assignment-based. The official guidance permits me to
reference other work I have undertaken in the workplace during the
discussion, so everything you produce is private preparation material
that connects my submitted assignments (the likely question anchors) to
the workplace evidence I will introduce verbally.

WORKING RULES (follow these exactly)
- Complete ONE step at a time. After finishing a step, stop and wait for
  me to type "continue" before starting the next step.
- ANONYMISE EVERYWHERE: internal security policy means I cannot name
  projects, systems or clients in the discussion. In every output,
  replace each real project name, system name, codename, client name
  and colleague name from my evidence with a consistent descriptive
  label based on what it does or their role ("the customer-facing
  booking service", "my team lead"), reuse the same label every time
  the same thing appears, and match the labels my submitted portfolio
  already uses where it has them. In the audit table, include a key
  mapping each label to its source item so I can trace it privately.
- No preamble, no restating these instructions, no concluding summary.
- If you approach an output limit mid-step, stop at a section boundary
  and wait for "continue".
- Before presenting each step's output, silently verify that every KSB
  code you cite appears in the list below, every claim traces to
  evidence I provided, and no restricted real name has slipped through;
  fix anything that fails before showing me.

THE KSBs, ORGANISED BY THEME
Theme A - Underlying Principles:
- K7: roles, functions and activities relevant to technology solutions in
  my organisation
- K6: lifecycle approaches and techniques, and their fit with my
  organisation's standards and pre-existing tools
- K11: nature and scope of common vulnerabilities (critically evaluated)
- K12, K14: data gathering, data management, data analysis
- K16: computer networking concepts
- K21, K22, K23: development lifecycle stages, the techniques used at each
  stage that produce artefacts, and development methods and the contexts
  in which each applies

Theme B - Technical Solutions:
- S4: initiate, design, code, test and debug a software component
- S9: security and resilience techniques
- S10: initiate, design, implement and debug a data product
- S12: plan, design and manage simple computer networks
- K13, S11: apply the principles of data analysis
- K24: interpret and implement a design compliant with functional,
  non-functional and security requirements
- K28: use tools that support teamwork effectively

Theme C - Innovation & Response:
- K8, S7, B4, B6, B7: how teams work effectively, applying organisational
  theories and up-to-date awareness of trends and innovations
- K9, K10, S8: leadership and management concepts as they relate to my
  role and how I apply them
- S20: responding to changing priorities and problems by making revised
  recommendations and adapting plans (distinction: demonstrate how my
  actions influenced team plans and outcomes; compare and contrast my
  responses across scenarios)
- S21: determining, refining and adapting software engineering methods to
  evaluate project outcomes

Theme D - Legal, Ethics & Landscape:
- K19, S15, B1, B2: applying legal, ethical, social and professional
  standards for both technical and non-technical audiences, in line with
  organisational guidelines (distinction: justify the standards applied)
- K20, B8: sustainable development approaches including diversity and
  inclusion (distinction: evaluate the impact of my organisation's
  sustainable digital technology practices)
- S23: extending and updating software development knowledge from
  professional and academic sources to inform best practice and lead
  improvements

YOUR TASK
Work through these steps in order and show your working:

STEP 1 - AUDIT. List every distinct piece of evidence found in both
sources. For each, give: a short title, the source (assignment /
workplace / both), the KSB codes it plausibly covers, and, for workplace
evidence, the internal competency indicator it originally sat under. Do
not invent evidence; only use what I have provided.

STEP 2 - PAIR. For each KSB grouping above, pair assignment evidence with
workplace evidence covering the same or overlapping KSBs. Prefer pairings
where the assignment supplies principles and comparisons and the
workplace evidence supplies real actions and results. For each pairing,
name the submitted portfolio item that serves as its anchor (the item
the assessor is most likely to build a question from). Aim for at least
two pairings per grouping where the evidence allows.

STEP 3 - REWRITE. For each pairing, write one merged answer of roughly
200 words (about 40 words per section) using this structure:

  Situation - brief context; weave in the academic underpinning from the
    assignment where it strengthens the answer
  Task - the outcome I was trying to achieve and my role, in my workplace
  Action - what I personally did, in the first person, naming the
    specific tools and techniques, using the exact KSB vocabulary
  Result - what happened and the impact; note which item in my
    submitted portfolio or workplace evidence supports this, if any
    (only reference items that exist in what I provided)
  Evaluate - compare my approach against at least one alternative,
    justify the choice, and say what I would do differently next time
    and why (this targets distinction-level criteria: justifies,
    evaluates, compares and contrasts)

Rules for the rewrite:
- First person throughout ("I designed", "I tested"), workplace framing
  first, assignments in a supporting role only
- Where the pairing has a portfolio anchor, open the Situation by naming
  it ("the [X] assignment in my portfolio covers the principles of
  this") and then bridge into the workplace evidence ("where I applied
  this in the workplace was..."), so I move from what the assessor has
  read into what I am introducing verbally
- Where a pairing has no submitted portfolio item behind it, mark the
  answer [NO PORTFOLIO ANCHOR] so I know I will be introducing this
  work unprompted, and make its Situation especially self-contained
- Use the KSB wording so the mapping is unmistakable
- Where evidence involved teamwork, credit the team but always return to
  my individual contribution
- Because I am introducing workplace evidence verbally with no artefact
  in front of the assessor, include concrete specifics I can speak to
  (my role, the techniques and tools, the sequence of what I did, what
  changed and how I knew), while staying within the anonymisation rule:
  specificity comes from actions and outcomes, never from restricted
  names
- Keep every claim traceable to the evidence provided; if you have to
  guess at a detail, mark it [CHECK: ...] so I can confirm or correct it
- Never fabricate results, feedback or metrics

STEP 4 - INDEX. Produce a prep table with one row per answer: Theme |
KSB codes | Portfolio anchor | Evidence title | One-line hook. Then
produce a second, reverse index: one row per submitted portfolio item,
listing the merged answers that extend it. This second index matters
most, because the assessor's questions will start from the portfolio;
whichever item they pick, I need to know instantly which workplace
evidence to bring in.

STEP 5 - GAP REPORT. List any KSB with no workplace evidence, or no
evidence at all. For each gap, write one reflective question that
prompts me to search my own recent work first, and that I can equally
take to my line manager, placement team leads, other apprentices, or
staff I have worked with, since they may remember work I have forgotten.
Phrase each question openly and reflectively, not as a yes/no check.
For example: "When have I been involved in setting up, changing or
troubleshooting a network, however small, and who was around who might
remember it?" or "Which pieces of my work have touched on data
protection or accessibility, and who did I work with on them?"

OUTPUT FORMAT
Present the results in this order, one step at a time as per the working
rules: the audit table, then the merged answers grouped by theme (A to
D), then the prep table, then the gap report. Use plain headings. These
are my private preparation notes for the professional discussion: each
answer should be self-contained so I can rehearse it and find it fast
during the discussion. They are not portfolio content.
```

A note on using the prompt well: the output is preparation material, not the finished article. Every apprentice must read each merged answer, correct anything marked [CHECK], and make sure they can talk about every claim without notes. If they can't speak to it, it comes out. The assessor is assessing the person, not the document.

### AI use and authenticity (read this before running the prompt)

Government guidance on end point assessment is explicit that AI must not be used to produce the portfolio. Your portfolio is already submitted, so that boundary is behind you, and nothing here touches it: the prompt produces **private discussion preparation notes**, the same category of material as the prep table the assessor-training video recommends, and preparing thoroughly for a discussion is exactly what you're supposed to do.

Two rules still apply:

1. The evidence is always real. The prompt forbids invention and marks uncertain details with [CHECK], but the responsibility sits with you: never let a generated sentence into your notes that you can't personally stand behind in the discussion. This matters doubly for workplace evidence you introduce verbally, because there's no artefact in front of the assessor; your credibility rests on being able to give concrete, checkable detail unprompted.
2. Declare, don't hide. If your skills coach or EPAO asks how you prepared, "I used an AI tool to index my existing evidence against the KSBs and drafted my preparation notes from that" is a perfectly good answer. Anything you'd be uncomfortable declaring shouldn't be in your preparation.

One reassurance on introducing unwritten evidence: this is permitted by design, not a loophole. The official guidance states the apprentice uses their portfolio to answer questions and may also reference other work they have undertaken in the workplace. Reflection and evaluation are expected in your spoken answers even though they don't appear in the submitted portfolio, so the Evaluate section of each STARE answer lives in your notes and comes out of your mouth on the day.

### Running the prompt in Gemini (or any other AI tool)

The prompt is model-agnostic, but a few habits get better results, particularly in Gemini:

- **Mind where the evidence goes.** If your organisation's security policy is strict enough that project names can't be shared with an independent assessor, the same policy almost certainly governs pasting internal promotion evidence into an AI tool. Use your organisation-approved AI tooling (for example, a Workspace or enterprise account rather than a personal one), and if in doubt, redact restricted names from the source files before uploading; the prompt's anonymisation rule covers the output, but only you can control the input.
- **Attach files rather than pasting**, and name them in your first message: "The file `portfolio.pdf` is Source 1. The file `promotion-evidence.docx` is Source 2." Long pasted text is more likely to get skimmed or truncated than an attached file.
- **Hold it to one step at a time.** The working rules in the prompt already require this; if the model rushes ahead or compresses steps, reply with "Stop. Redo Step [n] only, in full." Sixteen 200-word answers is a long output, and models of every flavour will try to summarise their way out of it if you let them.
- **Start a fresh chat for the real run.** Don't run the prompt at the end of a long conversation about something else; earlier context bleeds into the output.
- **Spot-check the KSB codes.** The self-verification rule catches most mislabelling, but skim the audit table yourself: if an answer is tagged S12 (networks) and it's about a database, fix it before going further.

### Companion prompt: mock professional discussion

Once the evidence bank exists, practising out loud matters more than more writing. This second prompt turns the same AI tool into a mock assessor. Paste it into a fresh chat along with the prep table and merged answers from the first prompt.

```text
Act as an independent assessor conducting a mock EPA professional
discussion for the Level 6 Digital and Technology Solutions Professional
apprenticeship (Software Engineering pathway). I will provide my
preparation notes (a prep table and a set of STARE answers mapped to
KSBs across four themes: Underlying Principles; Technical Solutions;
Innovation & Response; Legal, Ethics & Landscape).

Run the session like this:
- Ask ONE open question at a time, built from KSB wording, rotating
  across all four themes over the session. Do not announce which KSB or
  theme a question targets.
- Vary your phrasing so questions do not simply repeat my notes back to
  me. Include occasional follow-ups probing depth ("what were the
  alternatives?", "why that approach?", "what was YOUR individual
  contribution?"), because real assessors probe for distinction-level
  responses: justifying choices, evaluating outcomes, and comparing
  approaches.
- Wait for my typed answer before continuing.
- After each answer, give two or three sentences of feedback: which
  KSBs my answer evidenced, whether I spoke in the first person,
  whether I reached evaluation or stayed descriptive, whether I
  accidentally named a real project, system or client instead of using
  my anonymised labels (internal security policy prohibits this), and
  one concrete improvement.
- Every few questions, ask one question deliberately targeting a KSB my
  notes cover weakly, so I practise recovering when a question doesn't
  match a prepared answer.
- Keep a running tally and, when I say "end the session", summarise:
  themes covered well, themes to revisit, and my three highest-priority
  improvements.
```

### Verify against the assessment plan before circulating

A few details in this guide are deliberately generic because they belong to the assessment plan and your EPAO's kit rather than to us. Before this pack goes out, lift the exact figures from the current ST0119 assessment plan and add them to Section 2: the professional discussion duration and permitted extension, and the minimum number of questions. Also confirm which version of the standard each apprentice is on: the themed professional discussion described here belongs to the 2023 revision of the standard, and apprentices who started earlier may be assessed under the previous plan, which is structured differently.

